---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../consts';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="AI Audio Report is a small independent publication covering what music-production software costs and what its licences permit, with every fact dated and sourced."
>
  <h1 class="page-title">About AI Audio Report</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A small independent publication about the software musicians and audio people pay for:
    what it costs, what the licence actually permits, and which option fits the job.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Search for almost any AI audio tool and you get the vendor's own blog explaining why its
    product won the comparison it ran, an affiliate round-up whose prices were accurate
    eighteen months ago, and a post that answers a licensing question by guessing at it. We
    checked those pages. They contradict each other on price, and several of the licensing
    answers are simply out of date, because the terms were rewritten underneath them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beating that is not clever work. It is just the boring part: open the pricing page, read
    the terms, write down the date, link to both.
  </p>

  <h2>What this site can answer</h2>
  <p>
    Current prices, each carrying the date it was verified. What a licence permits, read from
    the licence rather than from someone's summary of it. The cost arithmetic that vendors
    leave as an exercise for the reader, like what an annual plan really saves or what a
    credit actually buys once you notice some things cost three of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    It cannot tell you how anything sounds. There is no test rig here and no shelf of plugin
    licences, so you will never read “we tested this” on this site. Where sound quality is the
    whole question, the judgement belongs to someone who did the listening, and we link to
    them and say who they are.
  </p>

  <h2>Who runs it</h2>
  <p>
    AI Audio Report is run and edited by <strong>Arnau Gran i Romero</strong>. Articles carry an
    <strong>AI Audio Report editorial team</strong> byline, and the ones he has read and checked
    himself name him as reviewer. If no reviewer is named, nobody has signed off on that piece
    yet, and you should be able to see that at a glance rather than assume it.
  </p>
  <p>
    How a piece actually gets built, and the things this site refuses to do, are set out on the
    <a href={withBase('/editorial-policy/')}>how we work</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p>
    No advertising, no sponsored content, no paid placement. At the moment there are no
    affiliate links either and the site earns nothing at all. When that changes it gets
    disclosed on every page it touches, and the
    <a href={withBase('/affiliate-disclosure/')}>rules are published in advance</a> so you can
    hold us to them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Corrections, tip-offs and “your price is wrong” emails are all welcome:
    <a href={withBase('/contact/')}>get in touch</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
